A||||3||batboy||oil pan removal on V8 Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||10-01-2000||09:25 PM||||Does anyone know if you can drop the oil pan on a Chevy small block V8 while it's still in the Fiero? If so, how big of a job (time consuming) is it? Looks like the old pan has been off once before and they reused the old gasket and gooped silicone sealer all over. I finally found where it's leaking and it looks like the gasket is cracked there. At this point, tightening it almost seems to make it worse. If the oil pan can be dropped without a major fuss, I'll replace the gasket. I'd like to get this car back on the road soon without a lot of work. I'm busy trying to get my 72 Chevy pickup roadworthy before cold weather too.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||V8Fiero||10-02-2000||08:36 AM||||I had to remove my one time. Had a leak below the timing chain cover. Wasnt too bad. The two bolts closest to the timing chain cover required some patience but other than that wasnt that big of a deal.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-02-2000||02:10 PM||||Don't fergit ta dreen yer oll ferst  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Seriously, if it turns out you <B>can't</B> get the pan off, you might be able to loosen the pan bolts enough to drop the pan... then carefully squirt some RTV in the area of the leak.  Partially tighten the bolts until it cures, then finish tightening. <P>Quick and dirty fix.  Obviously, removing the pan is the preferred solution, but the above might get you by for awhile.  Just a suggestion.       ||||reg||12
Z||000003||batboy||10-02-2000||08:18 PM||||Thanks Scott and Stu for the response. Scott, I was thinking about the same, didn't look too bad except the front couple of bolts. But, I like to get input first from someone who has been there before.<P>I have a new short block built for my Batmobile, but still need to save up for the new aluminum heads I want, so it'll be next spring at the earliest for that. I was hoping for a quick fix to tide me over.<P>Stu, don't think I haven't thought about doing exactly like you said and going nuts with the RTV, but if I can pull the whole pan off, I'd rather do that and put on a new gasket.<P>Oh yeah, I did pull an oil pan off a car ONCE without draining it first (not on a Fiero). What a surprise and a mess! Thanks for the reminder.||||reg||1
